1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,483
Welcome to Lecture One of AMCC 5160.
欢迎来到 AMCC 5160 第一讲。

2
00:00:02,483 --> 00:00:03,902
Welcome to AMCC 5160.
欢迎来到 AMCC 5160。

3
00:00:03,902 --> 00:00:08,406
I want to begin with a question rather than a product demo:
我想从一个问题而不是产品演示开始：

4
00:00:08,406 --> 00:00:10,889
when does an image become a world?
图像何时成为世界？

5
00:00:10,889 --> 00:00:13,372
A still image gives us a surface.
静止图像为我们提供了一个表面。

6
00:00:13,372 --> 00:00:14,791
Animation gives us time.
动画给了我们时间。

7
00:00:14,791 --> 00:00:20,643
A world gives us consequences: things persist, collide, remember, respond, and surprise us.
世界给我们带来后果：事物持续、碰撞、记忆、回应，并让我们感到惊讶。

8
00:00:20,643 --> 00:00:23,729
Generative systems are moving across that spectrum, but
生成系统正在跨越这个范围，但是

9
00:00:23,729 --> 00:00:27,276
the artistic problem is not solved by technical capability alone.
艺术问题不能仅靠技术能力来解决。

10
00:00:27,276 --> 00:00:31,780
Our job in this course is to connect two forms of judgment:
我们在本课程中的工作是将两种形式的判断联系起来：

11
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:35,575
how the system works, and why a creative choice matters.
该系统如何运作，以及为什么创造性的选择很重要。

12
00:00:35,575 --> 00:00:38,058
Look at the threshold in this image.
查看此图中的阈值。

13
00:00:38,058 --> 00:00:43,272
On one side is a bounded frame; on the other is a space that
一侧是一个有界的框架；另一个是一个空间

14
00:00:43,272 --> 00:00:44,691
seems explorable.
似乎可以探索。

15
00:00:44,691 --> 00:00:49,905
For the next two hours, we will use that threshold as our organizing metaphor.
在接下来的两个小时里，我们将使用这个阈值作为我们的组织隐喻。

16
00:00:49,905 --> 00:00:53,700
We will build a simple mental model of generative systems,
我们将建立一个简单的生成系统心智模型，

17
00:00:53,700 --> 00:00:58,914
practice reading their failures, and end by framing a studio experiment of your own.
练习阅读他们的失败，最后构建一个你自己的工作室实验。

18
00:00:58,914 --> 00:01:02,000
The opening question establishes the course's central claim:
开场问题确立了课程的中心主张：

19
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:05,901
a generated frame becomes a world only when change has consequences.
只有当变化产生后果时，生成的框架才成为一个世界。

20
00:01:05,901 --> 00:01:10,902
Persistence, causality, memory, and point of view matter more than surface polish.
持久性、因果关系、记忆和观点比表面光洁更重要。

21
